Suppose there are  $n-1$   $8$ 's between  $20$  and  $21$ , for some integer  $n\geq 2$ . Then, the expression becomes 
\begin{align*}
20\cdot 10^{n+1} + (8\cdot 10^n + 8\cdot 10^{n-1} + \cdots + 8\cdot 10^2) + 21 &= 200\cdot 10^n + 800\left(10^{n-2} + 10^{n-3} + \cdots + 1\right) + 21  &= 200\cdot 10^n + 800\left(\frac{10^{n-1}-1}{9}\right) + 21  &= 200\cdot 10^n + \frac{80\cdot 10^n - 800}{9} + 21  &= \frac{1880\cdot 10^n - 611}{9}  &= 47\left(\frac{40\cdot 10^n - 13}{9}\right). \end{align*}

Since  $40\cdot 10^n - 13 \equiv 40 - 13 \equiv 27\equiv 0\pmod{9}$ ,  $\frac{40\cdot 10^n-13}{9}$  is an integer, and since  $n\geq 2$ , the expression is at least  $\frac{4000-13}{9} = 443 > 1$ , so the expression is a product of two integers greater than  $1$  for all  $n$ , so it is composite, as desired.

