Decrease the whole by  $1$ . Now select  $2$   $2 \times 3$  rectangles and  $1$   $3 \times 2$  rectangle and add  $1$  to all. Thus we get  $2$  consecutive squares whose numbers are now successfully decreased by  $1$ . It is easy to observe that we can use this technique to remove all but the top row. Now we are left with  $1,2,3,\cdots ,9$ . We use the technique to make it  $0,1,3, \cdots ,9$  and then  $0 , 0 , 2, 4 , 5 , \cdots , 9$  and so on till we finally reach  $0,0,0, \cdots , 5$ .
Thus the number left in the remaining box is  $\boxed{5}$ .

If we take the following functions: $f(x,y,z) = z$ ; $g(x,y,z) = x + y + z = 7$ ; $h(x,y,z) = xy + yz + zx = 11$ then Lagrange Multipliers ( $\nabla f = p\nabla g + q\nabla h$ ) will produce: $0 = p + q(y + z)$  (i); $0 = p + q(x + z)$  (ii); $1 = p + q(x + y)$  (iii).

Equating (i) with (ii) gives us  $y + z = -p/q = x + z$ , or  $x = y$ .  This result now gives us the system of equations: $2x + z = 7$  (iv); $x^2 + 2xz = 11$  (v).

Substituting  $x = (7 - z)/2$  into (v) now produces: $[(7-z)/2]^2 = z(7 - z) = 11$ ;

or  $-3z^2 + 14z + 5 = 0$ ;

or  $(1 + 3z)(5 - z) = 0$ ;

or  $z = -1/3, 5$ .

which in turn yield two real triplets:  $(x, y, z) = (1, 1, 5); (11/3, 11/3, -1/3)$

Firstly,    $$ \frac{k(3k+1)}{(3k-1)(3k+2)} = \frac{1}{3}\cdot \frac{3k(3k+1)}{(3k-1)(3k+2)} = \frac{1}{3}\cdot \frac{(3k-1)(3k+2)+2}{(3k-1)(3k+2)}= \frac{1}{3}+ \frac{2}{3}\cdot  \frac{1}{3}\cdot \bigg(\frac{1}{3k-1} - \frac{1}{3k+2}  \bigg)= \frac{1}{3}+ \frac{2}{9}\cdot \bigg(\frac{1}{3k-1} - \frac{1}{3k+2}  \bigg) $$  Then, we have
\begin{align*} 
\frac{1\cdot 4}{2\cdot 5} + \frac{2\cdot 7}{5\cdot 8} + \cdots + \frac{k(3k+1)}{(3k-1)(3k+2)} + \cdots + \frac{99\cdot 298}{296 \cdot 299}  &=  \frac{1}{3}\times 99 + \frac{2}{9}\times \bigg( \frac{1}{2} -  \frac{1}{5} +  \frac{1}{5} -  \frac{1}{8} +   \frac{1}{8} -  \frac{1}{11} + \cdots +  \frac{1}{296} -  \frac{1}{299} \bigg) 
&= 33 + \frac{2}{9} \times \bigg(\frac{1}{2} - \frac{1}{299}\bigg) 
&= \frac{9900}{299}
\end{align*}

Let  $n = 2016$ .

Let  $p_1, p_2, \cdots, p_n$  be  $n$  distinct primes bigger than  $n$ . Consider the system of congruences:
\begin{align*}
a &\equiv p_1-1 \pmod{p_1^2} a &\equiv p_2-2 \pmod{p_2^2}  & \cdotsa &\equiv p_n-n \pmod{p_n^2}  
\end{align*}
Take a solution  $a$  of these system of congruences (it exists due to the Chinese Remainder Theorem), and we may assume this  $a$  is positive.
Let  $a_i = a+i$ , then  $p_i | a_i$ , but  $p_i^2 \not | a_i$ . Also, note that  $a_i$  is the only term that  $p_i$  divides, since if  $p_i | a_j$  then  $p_i | a_j -a_i = (j-i)$ , so  $j = i$  since  $p_i > n > |j-i|$ . 

To finish, let the product of  $a_i$  be  $P = a_1 \cdot a_2 \cdots a_n$ , and let  $b_i = Pa_i = aP + iP$ . Take any  $j \neq i$ , then  $p_j | b_i$  but  $p_j^2 \not | b_i$ , therefore  $b_i$  is not a perfect power. Also, the product of  $b_i$  is  $P^n \cdot P = P^{n+1}$  is a perfect power.

So  $\{b_i \}$  is an arithmetic progression which satisfies the desired conditions.

